## Why

XBRW++ is the third link in a chain of forks:

1. **[terminaldisclaimer/XBRecipeWriter](https://github.com/terminaldisclaimer/XBRecipeWriter)**
   — the original. It worked out the xBloom card byte format, the 32-byte
   signature, the CRC, and the dosage-times-ratio constraint the machine
   enforces. Substantial parts of `library/` still descend directly from it.
2. **[CrazyCoder/XBRecipeWriterPlus](https://github.com/CrazyCoder/XBRecipeWriterPlus)**
   — by Serge Baranov. A fork of the original, and the code this repository was
   forked from.
3. **[hessius/XBRecipeWriterPlus](https://github.com/hessius/XBRecipeWriterPlus)**
   — this repository, XBRW++, by Jesper Hessius.

Neither of the two upstream repositories carries a licence: no `LICENSE` file,
no `license` field in `package.json`, no grant in the README. Under the default
rules of copyright, each author therefore retains all rights to their own
contributions. GitHub's Terms of Service permit viewing and forking within
GitHub itself, but they do not grant permission to redistribute a work or to
ship a derivative of it outside GitHub.

That is why `LICENSE` is scoped rather than whole-repository. A fork cannot
license code it does not own, and asserting MIT over the entire tree would be
claiming to grant rights that are not this fork's to give away — which would be
worse than saying nothing, because it would mislead anyone relying on it.

## What is being done about it

Both upstream authors have been asked, politely and with the offer of a
ready-made pull request, to add a licence:

- terminaldisclaimer/XBRecipeWriter#4
- CrazyCoder/XBRecipeWriterPlus#1

MIT was proposed in both cases, to match the licence of essentially every
dependency this project ships. If both agree, this notice will be replaced by a
single ordinary `LICENSE` naming all three authors, and the scope note at the
top of `LICENSE` will go away.

